<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tirthankara">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tirthankara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tirthaṅkara, ford-maker, Jina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, a Tirthaṅkara, literally a &quot;ford-maker&quot;, is a supreme preacher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> who establishes a passage across saṃsāra, the cycle of birth and death. Jains hold that after attaining kevala jñāna, or omniscience, a Tirthaṅkara teaches and organises a fourfold sangha of monks, nuns and male and female lay followers. Jain cosmology says that 24 appear in each half of the time cycle, with Rishabhanatha first in the present one and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, traditionally dated 599–527 BCE, the 24th. Academic consensus broadly accepts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a>, the 23rd, and Mahavira as historical figures. Their teachings form the basis of the Jain canons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tirthankara.jpg" alt="Tirthankara" width="330" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jain_24-Tirthankaras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jains call these teachers <b>Tirthankaras</b>, “ford-makers”, who build a crossing over the river of rebirth.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jain authors told their own versions, in which Krishna is reborn in hell for his exploits but will one day be reborn as a <b>Tirthankara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Jain versions of the epic it is Lakshmana, not Rama, who kills Ravana, and Jain texts hold that Ravana will become a future <b>Tirthankara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jataka tales set many stories in the reigns of kings called Brahmadatta of Varanasi, and Jain tradition makes the 23rd <b>tirthankara</b>, Parshvanatha, the son of a Kashi king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a></li></ul></section>
